<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Adapter Pattern" id="adapter">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1200">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="ADAPTER DESIGN PATTERN - Bridge Between Incompatible Interfaces" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- DEFINITION -->
        <mxCell id="def_title" value="DEFINITION: Adapter is a BRIDGE between EXISTING interface and EXPECTED interface" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="definition" value="SCENARIO:&#xa;  Client expects → Interface A (e.g., JSON response)&#xa;  System provides → Interface B (e.g., XML response)&#xa;  &#xa;  PROBLEM: Incompatible! Client can't use Interface B directly.&#xa;  &#xa;  SOLUTION: Adapter bridges the gap!&#xa;  &#xa;  Client → Adapter (implements Interface A) → Adaptee (existing Interface B)&#xa;  &#xa;  Adapter IMPLEMENTS what client expects&#xa;  Adapter HAS-A reference to existing system (Adaptee)&#xa;  Adapter TRANSLATES/CONVERTS between interfaces&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;RELATIONSHIPS:&#xa;  ✓ IS-A: Adapter implements Expected Interface&#xa;  ✓ HAS-A: Adapter has Existing Interface (Adaptee)&#xa;  ✓ BOTH IS-A and HAS-A in same pattern!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=11;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="110" width="1520" height="280" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- REAL WORLD EXAMPLES -->
        <mxCell id="examples_title" value="REAL-WORLD EXAMPLES" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="420" width="760" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="examples" value="1. POWER ADAPTER &amp; SOCKET&#xa;   ┌──────────┐         ┌─────────┐        ┌──────────┐&#xa;   │ Power    │ ─────&gt; │ ADAPTER │ ─────&gt; │  Socket  │&#xa;   │ Plug     │         │         │        │  (Square)│&#xa;   │ (Round)  │         │Round○──□│        └──────────┘&#xa;   │  CLIENT  │         │  BRIDGE │        EXISTING&#xa;   └──────────┘         └─────────┘        INTERFACE&#xa;   &#xa;   Client has round plug, socket is square → INCOMPATIBLE&#xa;   Adapter has: Round socket (for client) + Square plug (for wall)&#xa;   Adapter bridges the gap!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;2. XML TO JSON PARSER&#xa;   Client wants JSON → Server returns XML → INCOMPATIBLE&#xa;   &#xa;   Client → Adapter (converts XML → JSON) → XML Server&#xa;   &#xa;   Adapter.getJson() {&#xa;     String xml = xmlServer.getXml();  // Call existing system&#xa;     return convertXmlToJson(xml);     // Conversion logic&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;3. THIRD-PARTY API INTEGRATION&#xa;   Your Company (expects format V1)&#xa;      ↓&#xa;   Adapter Layer (converts V2 → V1)&#xa;      ↓&#xa;   External API (returns format V2)&#xa;   &#xa;   If external API changes V2 → V3:&#xa;     ✓ Only Adapter changes (1 place)&#xa;     ✗ Without Adapter: 10+ components break!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;4. WEIGHT MACHINE (Pounds → Kilograms)&#xa;   Client wants weight in KG&#xa;   Existing machine returns POUNDS&#xa;   &#xa;   Adapter.getWeightInKg() {&#xa;     int pounds = weightMachine.getWeightInPound();&#xa;     return pounds * 0.45;  // Conversion!&#xa;   }&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;5. MOBILE CHARGER CABLE (C-Type ↔ Socket)&#xa;   Phone has C-Type port&#xa;   Wall socket is different&#xa;   Cable = Adapter (C-Type on one end, socket plug on other)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;COMMON THEME:&#xa;  Client can't directly use Existing System (incompatible)&#xa;  Adapter makes them compatible without changing either!" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="460" width="760" height="680" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- TECHNICAL EXAMPLE -->
        <mxCell id="tech_title" value="TECHNICAL EXAMPLE: Weight Machine (Pounds → KG)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="840" y="420" width="720" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="tech_example" value="PROBLEM:&#xa;  Existing product: WeightMachine returns weight in POUNDS&#xa;  Client requirement: Need weight in KILOGRAMS&#xa;  Can't modify existing product (already deployed)&#xa;&#xa;SOLUTION: Adapter!&#xa;&#xa;// EXISTING INTERFACE (Adaptee)&#xa;interface WeightMachine {&#xa;  int getWeightInPound();&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;class BabyWeightMachine implements WeightMachine {&#xa;  public int getWeightInPound() {&#xa;    return 28;  // Baby weighs 28 pounds&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// EXPECTED INTERFACE (what client wants)&#xa;interface WeightMachineAdapter {&#xa;  double getWeightInKg();  // Client wants KG!&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// ADAPTER (implements expected, uses existing)&#xa;class WeightMachineAdapterImpl implements WeightMachineAdapter {&#xa;  private WeightMachine weightMachine;  // HAS-A Adaptee&#xa;  &#xa;  public WeightMachineAdapterImpl(WeightMachine wm) {&#xa;    this.weightMachine = wm;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Implements what client expects&#xa;  public double getWeightInKg() {&#xa;    int pounds = weightMachine.getWeightInPound(); // Use existing&#xa;    return pounds * 0.45;  // CONVERSION LOGIC!&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// CLIENT&#xa;class Client {&#xa;  public static void main(String[] args) {&#xa;    // Existing machine (returns pounds)&#xa;    WeightMachine machine = new BabyWeightMachine();&#xa;    &#xa;    // Adapter wraps existing machine&#xa;    WeightMachineAdapter adapter = &#xa;        new WeightMachineAdapterImpl(machine);&#xa;    &#xa;    // Client gets KG! (adapter converted it)&#xa;    System.out.println(adapter.getWeightInKg()); // 12.6 kg&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY POINTS:&#xa;  ✓ Adapter IMPLEMENTS WeightMachineAdapter (IS-A)&#xa;  ✓ Adapter HAS-A WeightMachine (composition)&#xa;  ✓ Adapter does CONVERSION (pounds → kg)&#xa;  ✓ Client doesn't know about pounds (abstraction)&#xa;  ✓ Existing machine unchanged (open-closed principle)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;DESIGN:&#xa;  ┌────────────────────────────────────────┐&#xa;  │  WeightMachineAdapter (Interface)     │&#xa;  │  + getWeightInKg(): double             │&#xa;  └────────────────┬───────────────────────┘&#xa;                   │ implements&#xa;                   │&#xa;  ┌────────────────▼───────────────────────┐&#xa;  │  WeightMachineAdapterImpl              │&#xa;  │  - weightMachine: WeightMachine        │ ───┐&#xa;  │  + getWeightInKg(): double             │    │ HAS-A&#xa;  └────────────────────────────────────────┘    │&#xa;                                                │&#xa;  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────▼───┐&#xa;  │  WeightMachine (Interface) - EXISTING           │&#xa;  │  + getWeightInPound(): int                      │&#xa;  └────────────────┬────────────────────────────────┘&#xa;                   │ implements&#xa;  ┌────────────────▼────────────────────────────────┐&#xa;  │  BabyWeightMachine - EXISTING PRODUCT           │&#xa;  │  + getWeightInPound(): 28                       │&#xa;  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="840" y="460" width="720" height="980" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- ADAPTER VS DECORATOR -->
        <mxCell id="vs_title" value="ADAPTER vs DECORATOR (Common Interview Confusion!)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1180" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="vs_table" value="┌──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐&#xa;│  ASPECT          │  ADAPTER                    │  DECORATOR                   │&#xa;├──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤&#xa;│  INTENT          │  Make INCOMPATIBLE          │  Add NEW BEHAVIOR/           │&#xa;│                  │  interfaces work together   │  functionality dynamically   │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  PROBLEM         │  Existing interface ≠       │  Want to extend object       │&#xa;│                  │  Expected interface         │  without inheritance         │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  INTERFACE       │  Adapter implements         │  Decorator implements        │&#xa;│                  │  DIFFERENT interface        │  SAME interface as decorated │&#xa;│                  │  than Adaptee               │  object                      │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  CONVERSION      │  YES - converts format      │  NO - same interface         │&#xa;│                  │  (XML→JSON, Pound→KG)       │                              │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  EXAMPLE         │  Power adapter (round→sqr)  │  Pizza with toppings         │&#xa;│                  │  XML parser → JSON          │  Coffee with milk, sugar     │&#xa;│                  │  Pounds → Kilograms         │  Stream wrappers             │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  CATEGORY        │  Structural                 │  Structural                  │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  CODE EXAMPLE    │  adapter.getWeightInKg()    │  decorator.getPrice()        │&#xa;│                  │    → calls adaptee's        │    → calls decorated's       │&#xa;│                  │       getWeightInPound()    │       getPrice() + adds fee  │&#xa;│                  │    → converts result        │                              │&#xa;│                  │                             │                              │&#xa;│  WHEN TO USE     │  Integrating legacy code    │  Adding features at runtime  │&#xa;│                  │  Third-party APIs           │  Open-closed principle       │&#xa;│                  │  Format conversion needed   │  Avoiding subclass explosion │&#xa;└──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;SIMPLE RULE:&#xa;  Need to connect incompatible things? → ADAPTER&#xa;  Want to add extra features?         → DECORATOR" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1220" width="1520" height="500" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1760" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: Real scenario where you used Adapter Pattern?&#xa;A1: Payment Gateway integration. Our system expected StandardPaymentInterface (processPayment(amount, card)). Third-party gateway (PayPal) had different API (sendMoney(total, credentials)). Created PayPalAdapter implementing StandardPaymentInterface, internally calling PayPal API with conversion logic. When switching to Stripe, only added StripeAdapter — rest of system unchanged. Adapter isolated external dependency changes.&#xa;&#xa;Q2: Adapter Pattern for legacy system modernization?&#xa;A2: Legacy SOAP service, new microservices need REST. Created RestToSoapAdapter: exposes REST endpoints (what clients expect), internally calls SOAP service (existing system). Adapter handles: (1) HTTP → SOAP envelope conversion, (2) JSON → XML transformation, (3) Error code mapping. Allows gradual migration without breaking existing clients.&#xa;&#xa;Q3: Why not just modify existing class instead of creating Adapter?&#xa;A3: OPEN-CLOSED PRINCIPLE. Can't modify: (1) Third-party libraries (no source code), (2) Deployed legacy systems (high risk), (3) Shared code (breaks other clients). Adapter adds compatibility WITHOUT touching original. Also, single responsibility: existing class does its job, Adapter handles integration.&#xa;&#xa;Q4: Can we have multiple Adapters for same Adaptee?&#xa;A4: YES! Multiple clients may expect different interfaces. Example: WeightMachine (Adaptee). Adapters: (1) PoundToKgAdapter (for metric clients), (2) PoundToGramAdapter (for precise measurements), (3) PoundToOunceAdapter (for US clients). Each Adapter wraps same WeightMachine, provides different interface. Reusability!&#xa;&#xa;Q5: Adapter Pattern performance overhead?&#xa;A5: Minimal. One extra object, one delegation call. Modern JVMs optimize well. Trade-off: tiny overhead vs huge benefits (loose coupling, maintainability, testability). Can optimize: (1) Lazy initialization of Adaptee, (2) Caching converted results, (3) Batch conversions. Performance rarely an issue; flexibility gains massive." style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1810" width="1520" height="420" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
